<section>
  <p class="kicker"><%= link_to_page "guides", label_type: :nav, class: "cta" %></p>
  <h1>Switch on, charge and switch off the Go</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Recognise the GPS and battery messages, and know when the device is off.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">On the apron, and in the bag</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="waiting">Wait for <span class="highlight">the first GPS position</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Press the button to switch the device on and open the radar. Until it has a
        position, the ring says so. The message below the ring shows the
        GPS receiver&rsquo;s status: <span class="mono">GPS OFF</span> when it is
        silent, <span class="mono">MOVE OUT</span> when it sends position
        solutions with no date yet, and <span class="mono">FINDS FIX</span>
        once it reads a date from a satellite.
      </p>
      <p>
        That tells you whether to wait or carry the device into the open.
        The device announces its first position. The radar then shows the traffic around you.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "no-fix.png", alt: "The radar page with no position: the empty ring shows NO FIX and, below it, the receiver's progress" %>
      <figcaption>Waiting for the first GPS position</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="off">The logo on the screen <span class="highlight">means off</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Hold the button for two seconds to switch it off. The radio stops, the screen
        gets three seconds to draw the logo, and power is cut when you let
        go. E-paper keeps its image with no power at all, so the logo stays
        on the screen while the device is off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the same hold with a finger resting on the pad to leave the screen blank
        instead. An image left on e-paper for a winter leaves a permanent shadow.
        Leave the screen blank when you put the device
        away for storage.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "off.png", alt: "The screen of a device that has been switched off: the skyBlip logo, kept on the e-paper screen with no power" %>
      <figcaption>Off</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3 id="flat-battery">When the battery runs out, <span class="highlight">the screen says so</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        At 3.2&nbsp;V the device switches itself off. Its last message says the battery is empty. You pressed nothing, so the screen is the only explanation for the shutdown. Without that message, it could look as though the device had crashed or failed. The button stops responding at the same moment, because pressing it would bring the battery back to the same cutoff voltage.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <span class="mono">POWER BUTTON</span> (the default), plug in the cable to use the device again. The cable alone
        changes nothing on the screen: the charger&rsquo;s red light shows the
        battery filling. A press with the cable in starts the device,
        whatever the battery reads, and clears the message. If the battery ran out on a shelf, the device left no message.
        In that case, pressing the button displays the same flat-battery message.
        With <span class="mono">POWER CABLE</span>, connecting USB power after a flat-battery shutdown starts and charges the Go (see <%= link_to_page "power", label: "cable mode", anchor: "cable-mode" %>).
      </p>
      <p>
        At the next startup, the self test reports the flat-battery shutdown with
        <span class="mono">WAS EMPTY</span> beside the voltage. A phone that connects during that startup receives the same information, so a device that went dark does not come back as if nothing happened.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "off-flat-battery.png", alt: "The screen of a device that switched itself off on an empty battery: the skyBlip logo with the words BATTERY EMPTY under it" %>
      <figcaption>Out of battery</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3 id="cable-mode">Switch the Go on and off <span class="highlight">with the master switch</span>.</h3>
      <p>Leave your Go or Go+ plugged into the aircraft&rsquo;s USB socket, with its battery in. From the radar, press the button to open <span class="mono">SETTINGS</span>. Tap the pad to reach <%= link_to_page "controls", label: "the last row", anchor: "two-contacts" %>, <span class="mono">POWER</span>. Press the button once on that row to change <span class="mono">BUTTON</span>, the default, to <span class="mono">CABLE</span>. Set it on the Go, not in Briefing.</p>
      <p>When the master comes on, a Go that is off starts with one green pulse. On the Go+, you feel it too. The battery charges while USB power is on.</p>
      <p>On the ground, if USB power goes, the Go runs on its battery. The radar ring replaces its usual status or battery message with <span class="mono">OFF IN</span> above the minutes left, from <span class="mono">5 MIN</span> to <span class="mono">1 MIN</span>. After five minutes it switches off with three short red pulses. If power returns first, the countdown clears and the Go keeps running. Losing USB power in flight does not switch the Go off. It runs on its battery and waits until landing to start the countdown.</p>
      <p>The battery carries the Go through cranking. The five-minute wait covers time with the master off while the starter cools after a failed start. Rotax gives the 912 two minutes after ten seconds of cranking. Lycoming gives the IO-320 five minutes between attempts.</p>
      <p>Press <span class="mono">RESET</span>, the upper control on the left, to start the Go without USB power. The factory start-up software opens update mode if the button is held at start-up, so the button cannot wake the Go in this mode. Hold the button for two seconds to switch off. The Go stays off until USB power next comes on or you press <span class="mono">RESET</span>.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= screen_image_tag "cable-off.png", alt: "The radar page on the ground after aircraft power went off: OFF IN above 4 MIN in the ring" %>
      <figcaption>Countdown after USB power is lost on the ground</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off with <span class="mono">POWER BUTTON</span> (default)</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>starts, unless the battery is under 3.4&nbsp;V with no charger connected. In that case, the screen says <span class="mono">BATTERY EMPTY</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>does nothing. The screen already says the battery is empty. Connect the cable to recharge the device so you can use it again</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger in</th><td>charges and stays off. Connecting a cable in a flight bag does not make it transmit</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger into a device with a flat battery</th><td>charges and stays off. <span class="mono">BATTERY EMPTY</span> stays on the screen until the next press</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button and plug the charger in</th><td>starts and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RESET</span></th><td>restarts. Press twice inside half a second to start the USB bootloader, which lets you install firmware over USB</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off with <span class="mono">POWER CABLE</span></th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Switch aircraft power on</th><td>starts and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug in USB power after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>starts and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>stays off</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RESET</span></th><td>starts, even without USB power</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button while USB power comes on</th><td>opens update mode and waits there. Let go of the button and press <span class="mono">RESET</span> to start normally</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3 id="cell">Why the battery lasts the day.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Shutdown follows several steps in a fixed order. Before cutting power, the device puts its external flash memory to sleep. After the power cut, the memory could no longer receive that command. It would then draw its full standby current all night while the device sits in a bag looking switched off.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The radio and sensors account for the rest of the power use. In the air, the device transmits once a
    second. On the ground, it transmits once every ten seconds. Its address determines which of the ten
    seconds it uses, so aircraft on the same airfield spread their transmissions out.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Radio asleep before its reset line</li>
    <li>Shutdown follows this order: flash memory, receiver, power supplies, pins</li>
    <li>The device transmits at 1&nbsp;Hz in the air, 0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground</li>
    <li>The sensor hub&rsquo;s gyroscope is never read: it draws a milliamp</li>
    <li>Charging is refused outside 5 to 60&nbsp;&deg;C</li>
    <li>The light blinks and never stays on continuously</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="gauge">Read the battery gauge.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    With the cable in, the device measures the USB supply, not the battery.
    It therefore shows no percentage. The percentage is
    worked out on battery only.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It never walks backwards, and a 14&nbsp;dBm transmission that briefly lowers the voltage does not
    move it. A pilot stops trusting a gauge that jumps
    whenever the radio transmits.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>3.60&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is low, about 12%: the light blinks red, the radar says BAT and the percentage, and switching off leaves CHARGE THE BATTERY on the screen</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.50&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is critical, about 5%: the light blinks every 600&nbsp;ms. From here, the device saves nothing to permanent memory except the flight record, which provides evidence of the flight that is ending</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.40&nbsp;V</th><td>the device refuses to start on a battery this flat. The button stays disabled so pressure inside a bag cannot trigger repeated attempts</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.20&nbsp;V</th><td>the battery is flat: the device switches itself off and leaves BATTERY EMPTY on the screen. Each threshold takes three consecutive readings to enter and three to leave. A brief voltage drop during transmission cannot trigger a change, and a battery near a threshold cannot keep switching between states</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1.80&nbsp;V</th><td>below this, the reading comes from a floating voltage divider, not a flat battery. The device does not act on it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

